1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:07,107
Hello friends! In today's video we'll learn a very useful, practical Excel task.

2
00:00:07,672 --> 00:00:18,915
Suppose you have a sheet called All Data, with employee names, stock items, In
Quantity, Out Quantity and Amount — lots of data.

3
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:27,544
Now you want a separate Summary sheet where every employee and every
stock item's total is calculated automatically.

4
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:37,710
You won't need to apply filters manually again and again. We'll build the whole
summary report using formulas.

5
00:00:38,275 --> 00:00:42,136
Let's do this step by step in actual Excel.

6
00:00:43,223 --> 00:00:46,103
First, we have the All Data sheet.

7
00:00:46,668 --> 00:00:56,695
Column A has Date, column B Employee Name, column C Stock Item, column D In
Quantity, column E Out Quantity, and column F Amount.

8
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:06,988
Look carefully — Rahul Sharma's name appears more than once. The same
employee can also appear with different stock items.

9
00:01:07,553 --> 00:01:17,047
We don't want to calculate each row separately. We want to group the same
employee and same stock item and get their total.

10
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:21,943
For this we'll use a second worksheet called Summary.

11
00:01:23,030 --> 00:01:27,168
Now click the plus button below to create a new worksheet.

12
00:01:27,733 --> 00:01:29,675
Name this sheet Summary.

13
00:01:31,299 --> 00:01:35,053
Now we'll build the structure of our final report in it.

14
00:01:35,619 --> 00:01:46,072
Column A will have Employee Name, column B Stock Item, column C Total In
Quantity, column D Total Out Quantity, and column E Total Amount.

15
00:01:46,637 --> 00:01:52,610
Here we'll keep only the employee and stock combinations whose totals we
need.

16
00:01:53,176 --> 00:01:59,021
Remember, All Data is for the original data, and Summary is for our final report.

17
00:02:00,108 --> 00:02:05,313
Now in the Summary sheet we'll enter the employee and stock item
combinations.

18
00:02:05,878 --> 00:02:08,524
First row: Rahul Sharma and Laptop.

19
00:02:09,089 --> 00:02:11,798
Second row: Rahul Sharma and Tablet.

20
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:14,753
Third row: Priya Das and Mobile.

21
00:02:15,318 --> 00:02:17,729
Fourth row: Aman Khan and Tablet.

22
00:02:18,294 --> 00:02:20,875
And fifth row: Aman Khan and Laptop.

23
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:24,875
These are the lookup combinations for our summary report.

24
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:33,163
In the next steps Excel will calculate the totals from All Data automatically for
these combinations.

25
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:38,005
Now let's understand why we're using the SUMIFS formula.

26
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:43,413
In our example, Rahul Sharma has three Laptop entries.

27
00:02:43,978 --> 00:02:51,743
We want to add the In Quantity of these three entries, but not include Rahul
Sharma's Tablet records.

28
00:02:52,308 --> 00:02:57,791
Likewise, we want to select only that employee's and that stock item's entries.

29
00:02:58,356 --> 00:03:11,583
In SUMIFS we first say which values to add. Then the first condition, like
Employee Name Rahul Sharma. Then the second, like Stock Item Laptop.

30
00:03:12,148 --> 00:03:17,098
Excel returns the total of the rows matching both conditions.

31
00:03:17,663 --> 00:03:22,697
So we calculate the total based on two conditions at once.

32
00:03:23,784 --> 00:03:26,067
Now let's apply the formula in practice.

33
00:03:26,632 --> 00:03:31,966
In the Summary sheet select cell C3, because we need Total In Quantity here.

34
00:03:32,531 --> 00:03:34,579
Now enter this formula:

35
00:03:35,144 --> 00:03:47,859
=SUMIFS('All Data'!$D:$D,'All Data'!$B:$B,$A3,'All Data'!$C:$C,$B3)

36
00:03:48,424 --> 00:03:50,088
Let's understand it.

37
00:03:50,653 --> 00:03:58,824
First we selected column D of the All Data sheet, because In Quantity is in this
column. These values will be added.

38
00:03:59,389 --> 00:04:08,050
Next is All Data's column B, which has Employee Name. Excel will match it with
the employee name in A3 of the Summary sheet.

39
00:04:08,616 --> 00:04:16,893
Then All Data's column C, which has Stock Item. Excel will match it with the
stock item in B3 of the Summary sheet.

40
00:04:17,458 --> 00:04:20,765
A3 has Rahul Sharma and B3 has Laptop.

41
00:04:21,330 --> 00:04:26,962
Now Excel will add the In Quantity of only the Rahul Sharma and Laptop entries.

42
00:04:27,527 --> 00:04:29,362
The result is 190.

43
00:04:29,927 --> 00:04:35,474
See, in the original data 100 plus 50 plus 40 makes 190.

44
00:04:36,039 --> 00:04:38,428
So the formula is working correctly.

45
00:04:39,515 --> 00:04:42,353
Now we need the Total Out Quantity.

46
00:04:42,918 --> 00:04:47,163
In the previous formula we were adding column D's In Quantity.

47
00:04:47,728 --> 00:04:51,739
Now for Out Quantity we'll use All Data's column E.

48
00:04:52,304 --> 00:04:53,286
The formula is:

49
00:04:53,851 --> 00:05:00,016
=SUMIFS('All Data'!$E:$E,'All Data'!$B:$B,$A3,'All Data'!$C:$C,$B3)

50
00:05:00,581 --> 00:05:06,192
The conditions stay the same. Employee Name stays the same and Stock Item
stays the same.

51
00:05:06,757 --> 00:05:08,784
Only the Sum Range has changed.

52
00:05:09,349 --> 00:05:12,762
After pressing Enter, the result comes out as 45.

53
00:05:13,328 --> 00:05:17,936
So Rahul Sharma's Laptop total Out Quantity is 45.

54
00:05:19,023 --> 00:05:21,049
Now the third total is Amount.

55
00:05:21,614 --> 00:05:27,545
Amount is in column F of the All Data sheet. So we'll use column F in the Sum
Range.

56
00:05:28,110 --> 00:05:29,092
The formula is:

57
00:05:29,657 --> 00:05:33,988
=SUMIFS('All Data'!$F:$F,'All Data'!$B:$B,$A3,'All Data'!$C:$C,$B3)

58
00:05:35,266 --> 00:05:38,700
Both conditions are the same. Employee Name and Stock Item.

59
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:40,588
Press Enter.

60
00:05:41,153 --> 00:05:43,116
The result is 9500.

61
00:05:43,681 --> 00:05:49,889
In the original data 5000 plus 2500 plus 2000 makes 9500.

62
00:05:50,454 --> 00:06:01,014
Now our first summary row is complete. Employee Name, Stock Item, Total In
Qty, Total Out Qty and Total Amount all come from formulas.

63
00:06:02,101 --> 00:06:06,347
Now there's no need to type a separate formula in each row.

64
00:06:06,912 --> 00:06:10,261
Select the formulas from C3 to E3.

65
00:06:10,827 --> 00:06:16,629
At the bottom-right corner of the selection there's a small square called the Fill
Handle.

66
00:06:17,194 --> 00:06:20,032
Drag it down to row 7.

67
00:06:20,597 --> 00:06:27,637
Excel reads each row's employee name and stock item and calculates the
corresponding totals.

68
00:06:28,202 --> 00:06:34,423
Now see — Rahul Sharma's Tablet total appears
separately. Priya Das's Mobile total appears

69
00:06:34,423 --> 00:06:40,021
separately. Aman Khan's Tablet and Laptop
entries are also calculated separately.

70
00:06:40,586 --> 00:06:45,429
This way we completed the five-row summary report using formulas.

71
00:06:46,516 --> 00:06:49,929
Now let's add a Grand Total at the end of the report.

72
00:06:50,494 --> 00:06:52,564
In A8 write Grand Total.

73
00:06:53,129 --> 00:06:55,198
In C8 apply this formula:

74
00:06:55,763 --> 00:06:58,067
=SUM(C3:C7)

75
00:06:58,633 --> 00:06:59,593
In D8:

76
00:07:00,158 --> 00:07:02,462
=SUM(D3:D7)

77
00:07:03,027 --> 00:07:04,179
And in E8:

78
00:07:04,744 --> 00:07:06,963
=SUM(E3:E7)

79
00:07:07,528 --> 00:07:16,893
Now our total In Quantity is 515, total Out Quantity is 111, and total Amount is
37800.

80
00:07:17,459 --> 00:07:22,643
Remember, this Grand Total comes from adding our summary's values.

81
00:07:23,208 --> 00:07:33,896
If your source data is very large, you can also verify the totals independently
against the source data, as per your reporting needs.

82
00:07:34,983 --> 00:07:41,724
Now suppose we only need the employee-wise total and don't want to show
stock items separately.

83
00:07:44,374 --> 00:07:47,211
For this we'll use the SUMIF formula.

84
00:07:47,777 --> 00:07:51,574
SUMIF adds values based on one condition.

85
00:07:52,139 --> 00:07:54,102
In H3 write the formula:

86
00:07:54,667 --> 00:08:01,536
=SUMIF('All Data'!$B:$B,$G3,'All Data'!$D:$D)

87
00:08:02,102 --> 00:08:09,014
Here the first range is Employee Name, the second is the criteria cell G3, and
the last range is In Quantity.

88
00:08:09,579 --> 00:08:13,931
Excel will add the In Quantity of all of Rahul Sharma's entries.

89
00:08:14,496 --> 00:08:18,763
Now apply the formulas for Out Quantity and Amount the same way.

90
00:08:21,627 --> 00:08:24,059
Copy the formula down.

91
00:08:28,714 --> 00:08:36,436
See, Rahul Sharma's total In Quantity is 220, Out Quantity 53 and Amount
13100.

92
00:08:37,001 --> 00:08:40,031
Priya Das's and Aman Khan's totals appear too.

93
00:08:40,596 --> 00:08:48,212
So understand the difference: SUMIF is useful for one condition, and SUMIFS for
multiple conditions.

94
00:08:49,299 --> 00:08:52,499
Now let's give the final report a professional look.

95
00:08:53,064 --> 00:09:02,067
We make headings bold, adjust column widths, give Amount a number format,
and highlight the Grand Total row.

96
00:09:02,632 --> 00:09:11,188
Now understand a useful point. Our formulas reference the
entire columns of All Data. So if you add a new transaction

97
00:09:11,188 --> 00:09:18,867
below, it can also be included in the calculation, as
long as the data is entered in the correct columns.

98
00:09:19,432 --> 00:09:33,320
For large, regularly updated datasets you can convert All Data into an Excel
Table. Select the data, press Ctrl plus T, and confirm that the table has headers.

99
00:09:33,885 --> 00:09:39,901
An Excel Table can make managing data and adding new records easier.

100
00:09:40,466 --> 00:09:50,386
But remember, the employee and stock combinations in the Summary sheet
must also be updated if a new employee or new stock item is added.

101
00:09:51,473 --> 00:09:56,017
Before saving the final report, do a few important checks.

102
00:09:56,582 --> 00:10:03,174
First, the sheet name in the formula must be correct. In our formulas the sheet
name is All Data.

103
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:10,225
Second, the spelling of Employee Name and Stock Item must match in both the
source data and the summary.

104
00:10:10,790 --> 00:10:17,574
Third, the sum ranges for In Quantity, Out Quantity and Amount must refer to
the correct columns.

105
00:10:18,139 --> 00:10:26,331
Fourth, if a new employee or new stock item is added, don't forget to include
that combination in the summary.

106
00:10:26,897 --> 00:10:33,830
And fifth, verify that the quantity and amount columns in the source data hold
actual numeric values.

107
00:10:34,395 --> 00:10:38,875
These basic checks will make your summary report more reliable.

108
00:10:39,962 --> 00:10:46,234
So friends, today we learned how to build a second Summary sheet from the All
Data sheet in Excel.

109
00:10:46,799 --> 00:10:52,621
We used SUMIFS for the employee and stock
item combination total, SUMIF for the

110
00:10:52,621 --> 00:10:58,149
employee-wise summary, copied the formulas, and calculated the Grand Total.

111
00:10:58,714 --> 00:11:07,162
Now you can use this same technique in your office reports, employee records,
inventory, stock registers and sales data.

112
00:11:07,727 --> 00:11:18,479
If you found the video helpful, please like it, share it with friends learning Excel,
and subscribe to the channel for more practical tutorials like this.

113
00:11:19,044 --> 00:11:21,434
See you in the next video. Thank you!
